    "q-70716": {
      "dom_id": "step-q70716",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Consider the straight line given by the following parametric equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2t+1 \\\\ y=3-t \\\\ z=t+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind a vector that is parallel to the line.",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2t+1 \\\\ 3-t \\\\ t+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ -t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $(1,3,2)$  and is parallel to the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
