    "q-22318": {
      "dom_id": "step-q22318",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14117480/assets/58076d96d149ff4521103fd15c7187bc1a58595ab20f28be92be0f79385aee61.png)\n\nFind the parametric equations of the line shown in the figure above.",
      "worked_solution": "We know that the line passes through the points $P(-1,1,4)$ and $Q(3,-2,2).$ Now, we need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$,  as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ -3t \\\\ -2t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1+4t \\\\ 1-3t \\\\ 4-2t\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Therefore, the parametric equations are:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1+4t \\\\ y=1-3t \\\\ z=4-2t\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
