    "q-236988": {
      "dom_id": "step-q236988",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Fill in the missing parts in the parametric equations below that describe all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(6,2,1)$ and $Q(4,10,2).$\n\n| $x=$ | $6-2t$ | $t\\in [0,$ {{field-1}} $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y=$ | {{field-2}} |  |\n| $z=$ | $1+t$ |  |",
      "worked_solution": "First, we need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the line passing through the given points. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2t \\\\ 8t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6-2t \\\\ 2+8t \\\\ 1+t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nTo get the equations for the line segment, we take only $t\\in [0,1].$ Notice that substitution of $t=0$ in the above equation gives the coordinates of $P$ and substitution of $t=1$ gives the coordinates of $Q.$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ are the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=6-2t \\\\ y=2+8t \\\\ z=1+t\\end{aligned}\\,\\,t\\in [0,1]\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
